><text color="#9643FC" creationdate="D:20250702084221+10'00'" flags="print,nozoom,norotate" inreplyto="0767f34d-1540-457d-8d9c-6ec275eb7334" date="D:20250702084221+10'00'" name="61035346-f381-4147-bdae-2de8231a8199" icon="Comment" page="7" rect="307.289886,573.448364,331.289886,597.448364" subject="Sticky Note" title="Alexander Gibson"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.0pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Free Achilles tendon strains are highly task-dependent,&#xD;with peak strains being lowest for walking and highest for&#xD;running. The strain profile for exercises presented in the current&#xD;study may be used as a basis for the future design of&#xD;training and rehabilitation programs thatmaximize anabolic&#xD;Achilles tendon remodeling.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="7" rect="602.986023,505.448364,786.986023,597.448364"
/></text
